Two findings, both non-blocking, both worth fixing before anything depends on
them.

UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS is applied through ClientRequest.setTimeout, which is an
IDLE-socket timeout: the clock restarts on activity. The comment called it a
bound on the whole exchange, which is a guarantee this code does not have, and
overstating a bound is the same class of error as overstating a CSP. The
comment now says what the mechanism gives and states the residual — a
trickling upstream is not bounded by it, and that upstream is one the operator
named.

data-codev-approval-state computed three values, so a session-ended outcome
tagged as "refused" while the visible text and testid distinguished it.
Nothing asserts on the attribute yet, which is exactly why it was worth
fixing: the first test written against it would have inherited the conflation
this file exists to prevent. Four outcomes, four words, and a test that fails
when the branch is removed.
